  "title": "Q-RMEA ordinary context alignment: conditional statistical confirmation of original endpoints",
  "status": "active",
  "current_tier": "L4_statistical_confirmation",
  "claim_ceiling": "statistically_confirmed_advantage",
  "claim_scope": "For the trained frozen-encoder Q-RMEA output heads on official Re-TACRED, non-entity context weight-to-value alignment contributes to fixed-model task predictions. Population-level label-blind replacement degrades the predeclared CE diagnostic across all five seeds; the same models have established Macro utility over uniform pooling. Statistical confirmation is conditional on independent approximately normal training-seed differences on this fixed benchmark, for the sole conjunction of the original Macro utility and CE alignment endpoints.",
  "hypothesis": "The selected trained head has reproducible utility over uniform pooling, and its assignment of nonentity context weights to fixed word values carries useful task information. This does not attribute the full utility gain or a quantum-specific effect.",
  "result_status": "positive",
  "theory": {
    "self_consistent": true,
    "feasible": true,
    "assumptions": [
      "The frozen encoder features and official Re-TACRED split identities are common across all declared conditions.",
      "Train labels affect the fitted head through cross-entropy; validation selects the checkpoint; test targets enter evaluation only.",
      "The utility claim is scoped to the tested Q-RMEA output head and uniform pooling comparator, not internal Transformer attention or quantum hardware advantage."
    ],
    "implementation_plan": "Implemented Q-RMEA as a relation-conditioned word-weighting selector over frozen encoder outputs; fit the declared output heads for the complete five-seed contract and evaluate the selected checkpoints on the held-out test split.",
